Metadata: {"concept":"A camera obscura projects a softly luminous image beside a painter's canvas, presenting an optical aid as a possibility rather than established proof of Vermeer's method.","primary_subject":"vermeer_camera_obscura_hypothesis","secondary_subjects":["projected_light_image","artist_at_easel"],"visual_keywords":[],"composition_hints":["two_objects_in_interaction","clear_visual_contrast"],"scene_type":"interaction","mood":"neutral","palette":"teal_amber"}
Selected motif: A single simple seventeenth-century wooden box camera obscura with a brass lens and a pale ground-glass screen viewed from outside. Keep it an opaque closed optical box: DO NOT SHOW any projected picture, jug, light rays, cutaway or interior. No painter, easel, scenery, labels or text. This optical instrument accompanies a historical hypothesis, not confirmed proof Vermeer used it. Three broad flat colors and clean simple silhouette.

